Stephen you lucky is a documentary  filmmaker a former HBO studio executive  a feature producer and most recently  Dean of the school of film and  television at Loyola Marymount  University for eight years  last year he stepped down from LMU and  weeks later was accepted into the  Academy and joined the nickel  fellowships committee he is currently  working on a documentary on progressive  evangelicals presenting the award to  Sean Malcolm is Stephen you lucky  [Laughter]  I'd like to say that when I first  started out when I fell in love with  film I made documentaries and then when  I started doing feature as a fiction  work I still always found that films  that had a documentary basis or that  could have been could have been  documentaries were the ones that most  most interested me I look for example  the Battle of Algiers a film that could  take the reality of documentary yes  the reality the documentary doesn't yet  at the same time what you can't get in a  documentary a fictional film can supply  when I read mother I was absolutely  overwhelmed by the fact that I thought I  was seeing something that could become  as good a film as the Battle of Algiers  it is absolutely timeless work you've  got a sense of some of the drama in that  terrific scene that you just you just  saw and I just wanted to I'm very very  proud to be able to present the writer  of that script Sean Malcolm the nickel  fellowship award because I hope and  really pray and think that there's a  good chance that that film that script  could be made into a film because it  actually is the timing is perfect  absolutely perfect and for whoever is  interested in the film I know some  people are interested in it already I  would just urge that that hope that that  happened and on that note I'd like to  have asked Sean Malcom to step up  [Applause]  Wow Steve and I had no idea that you  were gonna equate me with something like  that and your words are so kind thank  you so much it's very hard to see all of  you out there but I know you're there I  have a few words I'll try to do it  quickly because I know we're on a time  limit but the truth is I do have a few  things to say so thank you for that  introduction thank you these actors that  was absolutely incredible you guys are  amazing for sharing your talent and time  tonight and breathing words breathing my  words our words into life for the very  first time when you write something you  don't have the benefit of anything other  than hear it in your head and you guys  are absolutely stunning thank you Geeta  for directing these scenes it's  incredible obviously I have to thank the  entire Nicol team Joan and Randy and  Chris and Melissa wherever you guys are  out there you're absolutely amazing this  week has been phenomenal and of course  all the readers and the Academy judges  who give their time every year and their  energy to this incredible fellowship we  could not do this without you and you  guys are so generous also I just want to  say a shout out to the nickel alumni who  spent their time with us this week they  shared their wisdom with us and that was  incredibly incredibly helpful of course  lastly but not least in terms of my  initial thanks we have to thank Donaghey  Nichol for the creation of this  fellowship this fellowship has been such  an inspiration to me and so many writers  around the world for so many years it's  an incredible gift and obviously an  incredible legacy so thank you to them  also I just want to quickly say thank  you to the Academy because I've always  really really wanted to say that  wherever the Academy is and thank you  specifically to the committee all the  members of the committee you guys this  this honor that you've bestowed on me  and the other four fellows this year is  it's really beyond comprehension I'm  absolutely humbled and also the other  finalists that were in our group it's an  incredible group of writers and I'm  absolutely stunned by all the talent and  just privileged to be part of this words  are never really gonna fully capture  what this means to me  but I am a writer so I figured I would  try imagine you've been slaving away at  this maddening pursuit called  screenwriting for 20 plus years and  you've really gotten comfortable with  the idea of obscurity it's fine really  total obscurity it's very comfortable  I'm good with it and then suddenly one  day you find yourself in the Academy's  headquarters on the seventh floor of  this very building you've just had lunch  with Eva Marie Saint in a room full of  legends and icons your phone is buzzing  because the producer is trying to get a  hold of you somebody who wants to make  your script you step out into the lounge  to return the phone call you're looking  at the panoramic view of the Hollywood  Hills and you find yourself staring  right at the Hollywood sign it's  literally it's ridiculous right it's  it's it's so on the nose nobody would  ever buy it the only thing that came  into my mind was a talking head song  once in a lifetime and you may say to  yourself well  how did I get here literally and then it  was oh my god what have I done that's  the magic of the nickel that's what this  is so how did I get here very quickly we  all take a different path  Aaron's path so straight and narrow mine  so circuitous if that's the way you  pronounce it circuitous thank you  so the truth is my journey began  twenty-one years ago in 1998 and in 1998  I actually submitted my very first  screenplay and ironically as evidence I  have my very first letter right here and  actually I was looking at this today I  mailed this script to 8949 Wilshire  Boulevard which is the building we are  standing in for I'm standing in you're  sitting on 21 years ago and yes for the  kids out there we did mail them in there  was not a thing called PDF so if you can  imagine thousands of physical scripts  coming to this building and how they did  it is just unbelievable shortly after  that letter was sent in I got my first  deer Shawn unfortunately your screenplay  did not advance to the quarterfinals and  so began a very long and twisted romance  from 98 to 2019 year after year I kept  writing submitting I tried dramas I  wrote a black comedy I wrote some  science fiction I did a ghost story I  did a techno thriller what do these guys  want from me  I even wrote a screenplay about a  screenwriter who writes a screenplay  about a screenwriter and one of them  dies along the way I forget which one it  was  it wasn't being John Malkovich trust me  mostly it went like this dear Shawn each  year the competition gets harder we  regret to inform you that your script la  blah blah blah Dublin but here's the  thing every single time I felt like  giving up a little piece of positive  reinforcement validation would come my  way from the nickel congratulations you  made the quarter with this script  congratulations  this one has advanced they would pull me  back in and then came mother and this  story unlike so many others it started  with a single photograph it was a  picture of a young boy in Aleppo about  the same age as my son who's here  tonight  he had just survived an airstrike just  minutes before and I knew I had to  somehow tell a story I didn't know who  he was and I probably never will but for  the first time in my life I tried  writing from my heart instead of my head  and that made a huge difference it's a  lesson as a writer that I will never  forget I know this is a celebration  tonight but it would not be right for me  to stand here and not say a word about  the Syrian people it's simply this we do  not have to accept what is happening to  our fellow human beings on this planet  we do not  every one of us can make a difference  together especially when we act together  if you want to help but you're unsure  how a great place to start is to simply  go to white helmet org or rescue org  they are doing work on the ground and  you with a few bucks can make a  difference let me just wrap up quickly  by thanking the people who've made a  difference in my life my mom Patty is  here tonight mom I love you I have no  doubt that my loyalty and love and  respect for all the women in my life  began with you I want to thank all my  friends and family that are here tonight  and around the world there's too many of  you that's a tree with many branches but  your love and support has meant  everything to me and I appreciate it so  much I especially want to thank my  beautiful wife Ivana she's here tonight  as well everyone knows I won the lottery  when I met you among so many other  things that I love about you your  instincts are flawless and so even  though we have a different taste in film  I know that if I write something I like  and you actually like it too I might  have actually done something different  very decent my two kids are here tonight  Julianna and Aiden guys are my pride and  joy I love you with all my heart and  being your dad is the best gift I ever  have and lastly I just have to thank my  dad Robert furo he was many things in  his life he was also an English  professor and an absolute connoisseur of  the written word and for a self-taught  writer like myself who never made it to  college having him as a dad turned out  to be a bit of incredible good fortune  even though we were so far apart  thousands of miles apart for many years  he read all my scripts he gave me his  feedback and he never once said a harsh  word or said anything about my stories  he only ever encouraged me  mother turned out to be the last script  that he would ever read he told me he  loved it and he was convinced that  something good was bound to come of it  and tonight proves he was right as he  usually was and so I just want to accept  this fellowship in his spirit because I  know it's his love of language that is  embedded in my DNA it is his love of  story that runs in my blood and it is  his kindness as both a father and